<% content_for :title, "Bipolar & Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-escaping-page bipolar-addiction-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-hero">
    <div class="addiction-escaping-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-escaping-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / BIPOLAR &amp; ADDICTION
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>&amp;</span>
        <span>ADDICTION.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-escaping-intro">
        THE MOODS. THE URGES. THE FAST DECISIONS.<br>
        WHEN YOUR BRAIN SAYS: DO IT NOW.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE OVERLAP -->
<section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-pink">

  <div class="ae-heading">
    <p class="ae-number">01 / THE OVERLAP</p>

    <h2>
      ANOTHER<br>
      NICE<br>
      MESS.
    </h2>
  </div>

  <div class="ae-copy">

    <blockquote>
      “Well, here’s another nice mess you’ve gotten me into.”
      <br><br>
      — Oliver Hardy
    </blockquote>

    <p class="ae-lead">
      Bipolar disorder and addiction can tangle together in ways that make it hard to
      tell what you’re feeling, what you’re reaching for, and what happens next.
    </p>

    <p>
      Sometimes drinking is about wanting a good time. Sometimes it is about shutting
      off a feeling, changing the speed of your thoughts, or getting a break from
      yourself. The reason can change from one day—or one episode—to another.
    </p>

    <p>
      Bipolar disorder affects more than mood. It can change your energy, sleep,
      judgment, confidence, and sense of urgency. Addiction adds its own familiar
      pull: the memory of relief, the routine, and the thought that this time will be
      different.
    </p>

    <p>
      When those patterns overlap, a decision can feel obvious in the moment and look
      very different later. That does not mean every choice comes from an episode. It
      means the state you are in can affect how a choice looks and how quickly you act
      on it.
    </p>

    <div class="ae-callout">
      THE URGE IS REAL.<br>
      THE STORY AROUND IT CAN CHANGE.
    </div>

  </div>
</section>


  <!-- 02 / MANIA AND URGENCY -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-yellow">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">02 / MANIA &amp; URGENCY</p>

      <h2>
        DO IT<br>
        RIGHT<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        NOW.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Mania can make an idea feel clear, exciting, and too important to postpone.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your thoughts may move faster. Your confidence may climb. Waiting can feel
        pointless, and other people’s concerns can sound like they just don’t get it.
        The distance between “I want to” and “I did” can shrink.
      </p>

      <p>
        Alcohol can slide into that momentum. It can seem like the obvious next move:
        more excitement, less restraint, no need to sit still with what you’re feeling.
        The idea of consequences may be present, but it can feel distant or manageable
        compared with what you want right now.
      </p>

      <p>
        The hard part is that feeling certain does not guarantee your judgment is
        working the way it usually does. An urgent decision can wait long enough for
        another perspective to catch up.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        “I’LL HANDLE IT LATER”<br>
        IS NOT THE SAME AS A PLAN.
      </div>

      <p>
        When you notice that everything feels immediate, treat that feeling as
        information. You do not have to settle the whole night, the whole week, or the
        rest of your life in the next five minutes.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / DEPRESSION AND RELIEF -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-purple">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">03 / DEPRESSION &amp; RELIEF</p>

      <h2>
        MAKE<br>
        THIS FEEL<br>
        DIFFERENT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        A long depressive episode can make relief feel less like a treat and more like
        an emergency.
      </p>

      <p>
        When the days keep feeling heavy, alcohol can promise a break: a different
        feeling, something to look forward to, a few hours when you do not have to be
        inside the same version of your life.
      </p>

      <p>
        The promise makes sense when you are worn down. But the relief is temporary, and
        drinking can leave you with worse sleep, more emotional turbulence, or another
        hard morning to get through.
      </p>

      <p>
        A depressed mood can also make the future feel closed off. “Nothing will
        change” can sound like a fact when you have been struggling for a long time.
        That feeling deserves to be taken seriously, but it does not tell you everything
        about what comes next.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        WHAT AM I TRYING TO GET AWAY FROM?<br>
        WHAT WILL THIS LEAVE ME WITH?
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        You do not have to pretend you feel hopeful. You can still look at what the urge
        promises and what it has tended to cost you.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / MIXED STATES -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-black">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">04 / MIXED STATES</p>

      <h2>
        MISERABLE<br>
        AND<br>
        RESTLESS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Sometimes the mood is low and the energy is still racing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mixed symptoms can bring painful feelings together with agitation, restless
        energy, or thoughts that will not slow down. You may feel desperate for
        something to change and driven to do something about it immediately.
      </p>

      <p>
        That combination can make alcohol seem like a fast exit. The urge to act may be
        intense even when you feel awful. You can be exhausted and keyed up at the same
        time.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is not the moment to assume that an intense feeling has given you a perfect
        read on what will help. The feeling is real. The urgency is real. Neither one
        requires you to let the first impulse make the decision.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        MISERABLE DOESN’T MEAN<br>
        YOU HAVE TO MAKE IT WORSE.
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / SLEEP -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-pink">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">05 / SLEEP CHANGES THE PICTURE</p>

      <h2>
        TIRED<br>
        BRAIN.<br>
        LOUD<br>
        IDEAS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Sleep is one of the first things to shift when your mood is changing—and one of
        the things alcohol can make messier.
      </p>

      <p>
        During mania, you may sleep less and still feel like you have plenty of energy.
        That can make your pace feel normal, even when it is very different from your
        usual baseline.
      </p>

      <p>
        During depression, sleep may not feel restorative, or getting through the day
        may take everything you have. Alcohol might seem like a shortcut to switching
        off, but it can disrupt sleep and leave you feeling worse afterward.
      </p>

      <p>
        When you are underslept or your sleep pattern is changing, your judgment and
        patience can take a hit. The next drink, next plan, or next impulse may feel
        easier to justify than it would after rest.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        NOTICE THE SLEEP CHANGE.<br>
        IT MAY BE PART OF THE PATTERN.
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / MOOD AND THE URGE -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-yellow">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">06 / WHAT IS THE URGE DOING?</p>

      <h2>
        SAME<br>
        DRINK.<br>
        DIFFERENT<br>
        REASON.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        An urge does not always mean the same thing. It can show up with boredom,
        loneliness, celebration, anger, exhaustion, a low mood, or the feeling that
        everything is finally moving at your speed.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask what is happening around the urge. Has your sleep changed? Is your energy
        higher or lower than usual? Are you feeling unusually confident, agitated,
        hopeless, or impatient? Are you trying to change a feeling—or keep a good one
        going?
      </p>

      <p>
        You do not need to diagnose yourself in the moment. You are looking for clues
        about what state you are in and what the urge is offering you.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        WHAT AM I FEELING?<br>
        WHAT AM I HOPING ALCOHOL WILL DO?
      </div>

      <p>
        Maybe the answer is “make this less painful.” Maybe it is “make this more
        exciting.” Maybe it is simply “I’m used to doing this.” Naming the reason gives
        you a clearer picture of the choice in front of you.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / BETWEEN EPISODES -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-purple">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">07 / BETWEEN EPISODES</p>

      <h2>
        THE MOOD<br>
        CHANGES.<br>
        THE HABIT<br>
        CAN STICK.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        Not every urge is a mood episode. Not every mood change is an episode.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can have cravings when your mood feels steady. A familiar place, a certain
        time of day, a memory, or the thought of relief can bring the urge back without
        mania or depression being the whole explanation.
      </p>

      <p>
        And a mood shift does not automatically explain every feeling or decision.
        Paying attention to your usual patterns can help you tell the difference
        between an ordinary bad day, an old habit, and a change that is starting to
        affect your sleep, energy, judgment, or behavior.
      </p>

      <p>
        The point is not to put every experience into a category. It is to get curious
        about what is actually happening instead of letting one explanation run the
        whole show.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        NOTICE THE PATTERN.<br>
        DON’T FORCE THE EXPLANATION.
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / MAKING ROOM -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-black">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">08 / INTERRUPT THE MOMENT</p>

      <h2>
        THE URGE<br>
        CAN WAIT<br>
        A MINUTE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p class="ae-lead">
        You may not be able to control the first thought or feeling. You can sometimes
        create a little space before acting on it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Say what is happening plainly: “I’m restless and want to drink.” “I barely
        slept, and everything feels urgent.” “I’m low and looking for a way out of this
        feeling.”
      </p>

      <p>
        Then make the next step smaller. Move away from the immediate opportunity to
        drink. Get through the next few minutes without making a bigger promise to
        yourself. Let the urge be there while you do something that does not escalate
        the situation.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is not about winning a fight with your brain or proving you have perfect
        control. It is about giving your judgment a little more time to come back online.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        YOU DON’T HAVE TO OBEY<br>
        THE FIRST VERSION OF THE PLAN.
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 09 / ACCOUNTABILITY -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-section ae-pink">

    <div class="ae-heading">
      <p class="ae-number">09 / ACCOUNTABILITY</p>

      <h2>
        EXPLAIN<br>
        THE PATTERN.<br>
        OWN THE<br>
        IMPACT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ae-copy">

      <p>
        Bipolar disorder can affect your judgment and behavior. Addiction can shape what
        feels familiar or relieving. Those things matter when you look at what happened.
        They do not erase the impact on you or anyone else.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can recognize that a mood episode was part of the picture and still take
        responsibility for the choices and consequences that need attention. You can
        regret what happened without turning regret into a verdict on your entire
        character.
      </p>

      <p>
        If something needs repair, keep it specific. Acknowledge what happened. Listen
        to the other person. Do what you can to make it right. Then look at what the
        pattern is asking you to change.
      </p>

      <div class="ae-callout">
        CONTEXT HELPS YOU UNDERSTAND.<br>
        ACCOUNTABILITY HELPS YOU RESPOND.
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-escaping-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="ae-ending-small">
        MOODS SHIFT. URGES SHOW UP.
      </p>

      <h2>
        SLOW THE<br>
        MOMENT DOWN.<br>
        <span>SEE THE WHOLE THING.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="ae-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Bipolar disorder can change your mood, energy, sleep, and sense of urgency.
        Addiction can turn relief into a familiar answer, even when you know the
        aftermath.
      </p>

      <p>
        You do not have to reduce every decision to one cause. Notice what state you are
        in, what the urge is promising, and what you want to protect when the moment
        passes.
      </p>

      <p>
        The feeling may be loud. The decision does not have to happen at its speed.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "ae-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>